1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,428
Why do bees build every room as a hexagon?

2
00:00:02,878 --> 00:00:04,815
Not circles. Not squares. Six sides!

3
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:07,734
Meet Bea, a very busy bee.

4
00:00:08,184 --> 00:00:09,671
Her wax is super expensive.

5
00:00:10,121 --> 00:00:13,294
She eats about eight spoons of honey to make one spoon of wax!

6
00:00:13,744 --> 00:00:15,754
So every wall counts. Which shape wins?

7
00:00:17,017 --> 00:00:18,658
Watch this. Try circles first.

8
00:00:19,108 --> 00:00:21,455
Circles leave gaps, like marbles in a box.

9
00:00:21,905 --> 00:00:23,903
Wasted space! And more wax to fill it.

10
00:00:24,353 --> 00:00:25,351
Bea is not impressed.

11
00:00:26,617 --> 00:00:28,656
Squares and triangles fit with no gaps,

12
00:00:29,106 --> 00:00:30,228
just like floor tiles.

13
00:00:30,678 --> 00:00:32,493
But they need more wall for the same room.

14
00:00:32,943 --> 00:00:34,101
So close! But wait...

15
00:00:35,350 --> 00:00:36,478
Now hexagons. Ready?

16
00:00:36,928 --> 00:00:38,507
No gaps, and the least wall of all!

17
00:00:38,957 --> 00:00:40,561
Each wall is shared by two rooms.

18
00:00:41,011 --> 00:00:43,879
A Roman writer guessed this over two thousand years ago.

19
00:00:44,329 --> 00:00:48,417
A mathematician finally proved it in 1999. Wow!

20
00:00:50,283 --> 00:00:51,719
Engineers borrowed this trick.

21
00:00:52,169 --> 00:00:54,033
They put a layer of thin hexagon cells

22
00:00:54,483 --> 00:00:56,477
between two thin sheets, like a sandwich.

23
00:00:56,927 --> 00:00:58,154
It's called a honeycomb panel.

24
00:00:58,604 --> 00:01:00,578
It's mostly air, but super hard to bend!

25
00:01:01,028 --> 00:01:03,068
So planes stay strong without getting heavy.

26
00:01:03,518 --> 00:01:05,791
It's in plane flaps, doors and cabin walls,

27
00:01:06,241 --> 00:01:08,163
and even in rockets and space antennas.

28
00:01:09,917 --> 00:01:11,229
Bees: less wax, more room.

29
00:01:11,679 --> 00:01:13,214
Planes: less weight, more strength.

30
00:01:14,450 --> 00:01:15,986
Would you build with hexagons too?

31
00:01:16,136 --> 00:01:18,155
If you love nature-inspired videos like this,

32
00:01:18,305 --> 00:01:20,319
like, share and subscribe to RoboticsInfo,

33
00:01:20,469 --> 00:01:21,667
and follow us on Instagram.
